WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.330
INTRO:
Ever since the turn of the millennium, we’ve

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:06.820
seen a massive trend towards free-to-use software,
games, and apps.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.670
Everything from Facebook, Google, and YouTube
to Robinhood, Clash of Clans, and Spotify.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:16.839
These services have become masters at alternative
modes of monetization whether that be collecting

00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:19.050
data or serving ads.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.169
From a macro perspective, these free services
are awesome as they have made access to information

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:28.169
and content easier and cheaper than ever before.

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:32.579
And in some cases, it makes no sense to spend
money on these services.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:37.019
For example, if you watch a random YouTube
video here or there, then it probably makes

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:38.809
no sense to buy YouTube premium.

00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:43.079
Similarly, in some cases, there may be some
pride in not spending any money.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.500
For example, maybe you want to max out your
Clash of Clans account without gems or maybe

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.300
you want to be a default god on Fortnite.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.360
Putting such scenarios aside though, if you
spend any amount of substantial time on these

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.570
free to use services, I’d argue that the
sacrifices that you’re making on privacy,

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:04.040
time, and the opportunity to be a customer
are far greater than any monetary cost you

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.260
could’ve paid instead, and here’s why.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.970
SACRIFICING PRIVACY:
Starting with the most obvious sacrifice you’re

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.640
making with free services, we have the sacrifice
of privacy.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.820
Nowadays, user data is one of the most valuable
forms of currency in the world, and this data

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.210
can be exchanged for billions of dollars.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.350
We all know that big tech and especially Google
collects loads of data on all of us, but have

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.140
you ever looked into what exactly this data
is?

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.800
Well, security.org did, and here’s what
they found.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.320
Starting with the reasonable data, we have
name, phone number, payment information, and

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.450
email address.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.990
These are all basic data that Google would
need to distinguish between users and appropriately

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.020
charge each user for subscriptions that they
may have.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.800
This is likely most of the data they need
to provide users with a great experience with

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.930
their services, but this is just the beginning
of their data collection.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.770
Moving on, Google collects data on emails
you write and receive, your stored videos

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:04.300
and photos, your stored documents, your stored
spreadsheets, and your YouTube comments.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.700
So basically, everything you store with them
and everything you communicate through them.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.330
Google has advanced machine learning and AI
algorithms that can comb through your photos

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.450
and emails which allows them to understand
your lifestyle, who you spend time with, and

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.640
your hobbies.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.840
For example, you might have a ton of pictures
of your dog.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.100
In this case, you would be a prime target
for dog food and dog toy ads.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.510
Similarly, maybe you have a bunch of pictures
at the beach.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.720
This would make you a prime target for flights
to beaches and luxury beachside resorts.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.590
Anyway, next up, Google also collects in detail
data regarding how exactly you’re accessing

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.590
their services.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.770
We’ve got an IP address, system activity,
data about interactions between apps, browser

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:48.450
type, device type, application version number,
carrier name, and operating system.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:53.650
In terms of location, we’ve got GPS data,
sensor data, and even information about things

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.490
near the device you’re using.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:59.430
And I haven’t even covered this activity
section which has another dozen data points

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.710
about your calls and interaction history.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:06.540
Clearly, Google collects data on basically
all facets of your life and this is the same

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.650
case with Facebook and Twitter.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.670
But what does this even lead to?

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.940
Well, the answer is low ROI for you and Google.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.600
BAD FOR YOU:
Advertisers aren’t stupid.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.900
They’re not gonna run marketing campaigns
that aren’t profitable.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.760
Sure, a startup might do this to generate
a base of users, but do you really think that

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.400
Apple or Nvidia or AMD is gonna run unprofitable
ads?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.080
No, and given all the data Google has collected
for them, their marketing campaigns are more

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.350
effective than ever before.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.900
This means that the average person will end
up spending way more money than they ever

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:43.910
need to.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.410
And I’m not talking about valuable services.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.790
There’s definitely going to be people who
get ads for things like Audible and SkillShare,

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.550
and they’re gonna get a subscription, and
they’re gonna get a bunch of value from

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.550
the service.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.349
Rather, I’m talking about predatory consumerism.

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:01.520
Buying a new phone that you don’t need or
going on a vacation that you can’t afford.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.390
Now again, if you’re doing well for yourself
and making $200 or $300,000 per year, this

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.560
doesn’t matter for you.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.390
You can afford to splurge on a new phone every
year and go on vacation regularly.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:16.509
But, for the average American making $50 or
$60,000 per year, these are expenses that

00:04:16.509 --> 00:04:18.850
simply don’t need to be in their lives.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.949
It would be much smarter for them to save
and invest this money into a house or an index

00:04:22.949 --> 00:04:23.949
fund.

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:27.699
And that might’ve been what they would’ve
done if it wasn’t for such effective marketing.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:31.889
Now, I’m not faulting companies for using
such advertising mediums as that’s simply

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:33.240
a brilliant business decision.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.059
But, from the consumer side, sure you didn’t
have to spend money to use Google; however,

00:04:38.059 --> 00:04:42.870
your net ROI is likely thousands of dollars
in the red.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.490
BAD FOR GOOGLE:
At the same time, it’s not like Google is

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.400
making much money off of you individually
either.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.770
Last year, Google pulled in a total of $209,497,000,000
from advertising.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:59.289
This includes Google search, YouTube, and
all other sources of Google-related advertising.

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:04.289
Now, that is an absurd amount of revenue,
but you have to consider that Google also

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:06.370
has an absurd amount of customers.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.370
Literally over half the world uses Google
with their worldwide user count coming in

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.339
at 4.3 billion.

00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:17.830
If we divide their advertising revenue by
their user count, we get just $48.72 and that’s

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:18.840
revenue.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.900
Google’s net income margin sits at just
under 30% meaning that less than $15 of that

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.389
is actually profit.

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:28.460
Now, the real numbers are likely much more
skewed.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:33.120
There are likely billions of users that only
profit Google $1 or $2 per year, and there’s

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.469
likely billions who profit them $50 or $60
per year.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:38.340
But I think you get the point.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.610
Google isn’t making hundreds or thousands
of dollars from each customer.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.470
But, by collecting data, a lot of their users
end up spending thousands of dollars that

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.960
they don’t need to be spending.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.809
So, wouldn’t it be a lot better if we cut
out advertisers and directly paid Google?

00:05:51.809 --> 00:05:55.340
What if we paid Google $20 per month for all
of their services.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:00.139
That’s $240 per year, but in return, we
get an ad-free data collection free private

00:06:00.139 --> 00:06:02.240
experience across all Google services.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.580
Not only would that provide a far superior
user experience, but it would save the average

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.110
person thousands of dollars in unnecessary
spending.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:13.740
In the meantime, since Google doesn’t have
to spend money on appealing to or coordinating

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.280
advertisers, they can enjoy substantially
more revenue and higher profit margins.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.479
It’s a win-win scenario.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:23.210
And if you’re not convinced by potential
financial savings or a better user experience

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:28.550
or more privacy, I think you should strongly
consider the savings in time.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.409
SACRIFICING TIME:
While all the benefits we’ve discussed so

00:06:32.409 --> 00:06:37.659
far are definitely valuable, the dealbreaker
for me personally is definitely time.

00:06:37.659 --> 00:06:42.020
Most people rarely consider time as a currency
which is a shame given that it’s the most

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.889
valuable currency we have.

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:47.990
And when we use free services, the currency
that we’re often paying with is time.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:51.419
Again, this isn’t as important if you barely
use free services.

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:56.500
For example, if you only spend 20 minutes
on YouTube every day, it’s not a big deal.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:02.089
But, I know a lot of us spend 6, 7, 9 hours
on YouTube every single day myself included.

00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:06.639
If we’re not working or socializing, we’re
probably watching YouTube.

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:11.080
And nowadays, the standard on YouTube has
become two 5 second non-skippable ads roughly

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.580
every 3 minutes.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.939
This means that for every hour you spend on
YouTube, you’re consuming 40 5 second ads

00:07:16.939 --> 00:07:19.159
or 200 seconds worth of ads.

00:07:19.159 --> 00:07:23.130
If you watch 3 hours worth of YouTube on a
daily basis, you’re consuming 600 seconds

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:26.629
worth of ads every day and 18,000 seconds
worth of ads every month.

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:31.199
That’s the same as 5 hours worth of ads
every single month.

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:34.339
YouTube Premium’s individual plan costs
$11.99 per month.

00:07:34.339 --> 00:07:38.509
This means that by choosing to watch ads instead
of paying for YouTube premium, you’re effectively

00:07:38.509 --> 00:07:41.529
selling your time for $2.40 per hour.

00:07:41.529 --> 00:07:43.879
And that’s if you’re being stupid with
the subscription.

00:07:43.879 --> 00:07:48.949
The smarter play is to obviously get the family
plan and split it with your friends, family,

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:49.979
and/or roommates.

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:54.779
The family plan costs $18 per month and allows
you to add up to 5 members onto the plan.

00:07:54.779 --> 00:07:58.759
This means that each member is effectively
paying just $3 per month.

00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:02.270
Considering this, by choosing to watch ads,
you’re effectively selling your time for

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.120
$0.60 per hour.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:07.580
Even if you’re watching just 1 hour worth
of YouTube per day, you’re selling your

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:10.460
time for $1.80 per hour.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.529
If you’re watching 6 hours, you’re looking
at $0.30 per hour and if you’re watching

00:08:14.529 --> 00:08:17.909
9 hours, you’re looking at $0.20 per hour.

00:08:17.909 --> 00:08:22.139
I don’t think I need to explain why these
are unfavorable tradeoffs.

00:08:22.139 --> 00:08:25.960
And similar arguments can be made if you spend
your time scrolling through TikTok or Instagram

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:26.960
instead.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.259
By using these free services, you’re not
only selling your time for laughable amounts

00:08:31.259 --> 00:08:36.110
but you’re spending hours every month watching
ads instead of watching the content itself.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:41.020
MISALIGNED INTERESTS:
If you thought sacrificing your privacy, your

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.949
user experience, and your time were already
not bad enough, you’re also sacrificing

00:08:44.949 --> 00:08:46.699
your position as a customer.

00:08:46.699 --> 00:08:49.080
For example, take a look at these actions.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.170
A few years ago, YouTube moved away from edgy
content.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.200
Last year, Robinhood halted trading on GameStop.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.600
And just a few months ago, YouTube removed
the dislike button.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:03.270
All of these actions were strongly disapproved
by users and made users feel like these companies

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:04.660
weren’t listening to their customers.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:09.630
But, in reality, all of these companies were
listening to their customers and swiftly taking

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:10.630
action.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:12.990
The problem is that you’re not the customer.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:17.230
With YouTube, advertisers are the customer
and they wanted less edgy content.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:21.639
And with Robinhood, the market makers were
the customers, and they weren’t fans of

00:09:21.639 --> 00:09:22.639
GameStop.

00:09:22.639 --> 00:09:26.050
In both of these cases, even though you’re
a user, you’re actually the product being

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.050
sold.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.680
Now, imagine if everyone watching YouTube
had a YouTube premium subscription.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.140
Do you think YouTube would’ve removed the
disklike button or moved away from edgy content?

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.140
No.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:40.160
Similarly, if Robinhood customers paid a commission
on their trades like with a traditional brokerage,

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.120
do you think GameStop would’ve been halted?

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.120
Probably not.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.260
But, by choosing to be the product, we give
up all the power we could have as a customer.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:52.540
Now, look at the flip side of the situation
at companies with extremely favorable reputations.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:55.240
Probably one of the most admired companies
in the world is Apple.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:59.180
Apple does have their fair share of flaws
when it comes to overpricing and anti-repair.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:03.620
But, Apple receives virtually no complaints
regarding the quality of their products or

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.740
the quality of their services or their user
experience or the security of their devices

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.949
or the privacy that Apple provides.

00:10:09.949 --> 00:10:13.279
And this is because, with Apple, you’re
truly the customer.

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:21.380
THE TRUTH:
At the end of the day, many companies have

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.710
flocked over to free-to-use models because
it gives them extraordinary reach.

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:29.790
This is truly awesome because it has made
great services accessible to people all around

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:30.790
the world.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.690
And I strongly believe that their net impact
on the world is way more positive than negative.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:39.140
With that being said though, there is no such
thing as free services.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:43.379
If you’re not paying with cash, you’re
paying with data and time which is oftentimes

00:10:43.379 --> 00:10:45.079
much more expensive.

00:10:45.079 --> 00:10:49.690
I think companies should offer more paid options
that allow users to become customers.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:53.339
Not only would this be more profitable for
companies, but it would allow them to earn

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:54.579
a much better reputation.

00:10:54.579 --> 00:10:58.360
Do you really think Google wants to be infamous
for collecting data?

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:03.540
When Sergey Brin and Larry Page founded Google,
their slogan was literally “Don’t Be Evil”.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.290
So, I think basically every company would
prefer to directly deal with their users.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:11.630
The problem is that there doesn’t seem to
be enough interest from us for companies to

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.310
take this option more seriously.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.529
So, in the end, the choice comes down to us.

00:11:16.529 --> 00:11:20.420
Do we want to remain the product or would
we rather become the customer?

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:22.060
Where do you guys stand on this spectrum?

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:23.120
Comment that down below.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.839
Also, drop a like if this video made you look
at the situation with big tech differently.

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:31.660
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:37.600
and consider subscribing to see more questions
